-- Q1 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · easy · Skill 2 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans A] (g83)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt83$Glycine has only two ionizable groups: an \(\alpha\)-carboxyl with \(\text{pK}_a = 2.34\) and an \(\alpha\)-amino with \(\text{pK}_a = 9.60\). What is its isoelectric point \(\text{pI}\)?$qt83$,
    $op83$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pI} = 5.97\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pI} = 7.00\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pI} = 9.60\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pI} = 11.94\\)"}]$op83$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex83$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is A because for an amino acid whose only ionizable groups are the backbone \(\alpha\)-carboxyl and \(\alpha\)-amino (a neutral, non-ionizable side chain), the isoelectric point is the average of the two flanking \(\text{pK}_a\) values that bracket the net-neutral zwitterion. Working it out: \(\text{pI} = \frac{\text{pK}_a1 + \text{pK}_a2}{2} = \frac{2.34 + 9.60}{2} = \frac{11.94}{2} = 5.97\). At this pH the population is dominated by the zwitterion (protonated \(-\text{NH}_3^+\) and deprotonated \(-\text{COO}^-\)), giving zero average net charge. (Choice B) \(\text{pI} = 7.00\) reflects the misconception that the isoelectric point is always neutral pH; pI depends on the specific \(\text{pK}_a\) values of the molecule, and for glycine it is acidic (5.97), not 7.00. (Choice C) \(\text{pI} = 9.60\) uses a single \(\text{pK}_a\) (the \(\alpha\)-amino value) rather than averaging the two relevant values, so it skips the required averaging step. (Choice D) \(\text{pI} = 11.94\) is the sum \(2.34 + 9.60\) without dividing by two, an incomplete execution of the average. Skill 2$ex83$,
    'easy', '5D', $cc83$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc83$, 'biochemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a83B$pI is always neutral pH 7$a83B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a83C$uses a single pKa instead of averaging the two flanking values$a83C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a83D$sums the two pKa values but omits dividing by two$a83D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · easy · Skill 2 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans B] (g84)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt84$Alanine has \(\text{pK}_a\) values of 2.34 (\(\alpha\)-carboxyl) and 9.69 (\(\alpha\)-amino), giving \(\text{pI}\approx 6.0\). A solution of free alanine is buffered to pH 9.0. What is the sign of alanine's overall (average) net charge under these conditions?$qt84$,
    $op84$[{"label":"A","text":"Net positive, because both ionizable groups remain protonated at pH 9.0"},{"label":"B","text":"Net negative, because the pH lies above the isoelectric point"},{"label":"C","text":"Net zero, because pH 9.0 falls within the buffering range of the molecule"},{"label":"D","text":"Net positive, because pH above the \\(\\text{pI}\\) increasingly protonates the molecule"}]$op84$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex84$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is B because at any pH greater than the isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)), an amino acid carries a net negative charge, and pH 9.0 is well above alanine's \(\text{pI}\) of 6.0. The \(\text{pI}\) is the pH of zero net charge; raising the pH above it deprotonates ionizable groups, removing positive charge and exposing negative charge. At pH 9.0 the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) 2.34) is essentially fully deprotonated (\(\text{-COO}^-\), charge \(-1\)), while the \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a\) 9.69) is partially protonated: since pH 9.0 is below 9.69, slightly more than half of the amino groups still carry \(\text{-NH}_3^+\). Combining a full \(-1\) from the carboxylate with a fractional positive (between \(0\) and \(+1\)) from the amino group yields a net charge between \(-1\) and \(0\) — a net negative value. The qualitative rule (pH > pI gives net negative) is sufficient and matches the arithmetic. (Choice A) At pH 9.0 the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) 2.34) is far above its \(\text{pK}_a\) and is therefore deprotonated, not protonated, so the claim that both groups remain protonated is incorrect and would not produce a net positive charge. (Choice C) Net zero charge occurs only at the \(\text{pI}\) (~6.0), not at pH 9.0; the existence of a local buffering region near a \(\text{pK}_a\) does not make the net charge zero. (Choice D) This reverses the relationship: raising the pH above the \(\text{pI}\) drives deprotonation (loss of \(\text{H}^+\)), making the molecule more negative, not more positive. Skill 2$ex84$,
    'easy', '5D', $cc84$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc84$, 'biochemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a84A$Fully protonated cation: assumes both groups stay protonated regardless of pH$a84A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a84C$Assumes zero net charge wherever the molecule buffers, conflating buffering range with the pI$a84C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a84D$Reverses pH-charge relationship: treats pH above pI as causing protonation and net positive charge$a84D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · medium · Skill 2 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans C] (g85)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt85$Valine has \(\text{pK}_{a1} \approx 2.3\) (\(\alpha\)-carboxyl) and \(\text{pK}_{a2} \approx 9.6\) (\(\alpha\)-amino). A sample is dissolved in \(0.1\ \text{M}\) HCl at \(\text{pH} \approx 1\). Which protonation form predominates?$qt85$,
    $op85$[{"label":"A","text":"The zwitterion, with \\(\\text{-COO}^-\\) and \\(\\text{-NH}_3^+\\) giving a net charge of \\(0\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"The fully deprotonated anion, with \\(\\text{-COO}^-\\) and \\(\\text{-NH}_2\\) giving a net charge of \\(-1\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"The fully protonated cation, with \\(\\text{-COOH}\\) and \\(\\text{-NH}_3^+\\) giving a net charge of \\(+1\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"An equal 50/50 mixture of the cation and the zwitterion, because \\(\\text{pH}\\) sits at a buffering plateau"}]$op85$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex85$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is C because at \(\text{pH} \approx 1\) the solution is well below both ionizable \(\text{pK}_a\) values, so both groups stay protonated. A group is protonated when \(\text{pH} < \text{pK}_a\) and deprotonated when \(\text{pH} > \text{pK}_a\). At \(\text{pH} \approx 1\): for the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl, \(1 < 2.3\), so it remains \(\text{-COOH}\) (neutral); for the \(\alpha\)-amino, \(1 \ll 9.6\), so it remains \(\text{-NH}_3^+\) (\(+1\)). Net charge \(= 0 + (+1) = +1\), the fully protonated cation. (Choice A) The zwitterion (\(\text{-COO}^-/\text{-NH}_3^+\), net \(0\)) predominates only in the intermediate region between the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values, centered on the isoelectric point \(\text{pI} = (2.3 + 9.6)/2 \approx 5.95\), not at \(\text{pH} \approx 1\). (Choice B) The fully deprotonated anion (\(\text{-COO}^-/\text{-NH}_2\), net \(-1\)) predominates only well above \(\text{pK}_{a2}\) (\(\text{pH} > 9.6\)), the opposite extreme of the titration. (Choice D) An equal 50/50 mixture of two forms occurs only when \(\text{pH}\) equals one of the \(\text{pK}_a\) values (the buffering midpoints near \(2.3\) or \(9.6\)); at \(\text{pH} \approx 1\) the carboxyl is overwhelmingly protonated, not half-titrated. Skill 2$ex85$,
    'medium', '5D', $cc85$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc85$, 'biochemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a85A$Defaulting to the zwitterion as the amino acid's form at all pH values rather than recognizing it dominates only near the pI$a85A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a85B$Treating the acidic extreme as if it were the basic extreme, picking the fully deprotonated form for a low pH$a85B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a85D$Confusing a buffering midpoint (pH = pKa, 50/50 of two species) with a pH far from any pKa where one species dominates$a85D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · medium · Skill 4 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans D] (g86)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt86$Free glutamate carries an \(\alpha\)-carboxyl group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 2.1\)), a side-chain carboxyl group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 4.1\)), and an \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 9.5\)). What is its isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\))?$qt86$,
    $op86$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 5.2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 6.8\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 5.8\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 3.1\\)"}]$op86$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex86$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is D because the \(\text{pI}\) is the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the net-neutral (zwitterionic) species, and for an acidic amino acid like glutamate those are the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values. As pH rises from very acidic, glutamate moves from a net charge of \(+1\) (all groups protonated) to \(0\) after the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl deprotonates at \(\text{pK}_a \approx 2.1\), then to \(-1\) after the side-chain carboxyl deprotonates at \(\text{pK}_a \approx 4.1\). The neutral form therefore exists between these two ionizations, so \(\text{pI} = (2.1 + 4.1)/2 = 6.2/2 = 3.1\). The basic \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 9.5\)) deprotonates far above the neutral point and does not bracket the zwitterion, so it is excluded from the average. (Choice A) \(\approx 5.2\) is the mean of all three \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.1 + 4.1 + 9.5)/3\); the \(\text{pI}\) averages only the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values adjacent to the neutral species, never all ionizable groups. (Choice B) \(\approx 6.8\) is the average of the two highest \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((4.1 + 9.5)/2\); this would identify the \(\text{pI}\) if the zwitterion were flanked by those groups, which is true for a basic (not acidic) amino acid. (Choice C) \(\approx 5.8\) averages the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl and \(\alpha\)-amino groups, \((2.1 + 9.5)/2\), incorrectly treating glutamate as if it had no ionizable side chain; the acidic side chain is the group whose \(\text{pK}_a\) must be used. Skill 4$ex86$,
    'medium', '5D', $cc86$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc86$, 'biochemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a86A$averages all three pKa values instead of the two flanking the neutral species$a86A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a86B$averages the two highest pKa values, treating the acidic amino acid as if it were basic$a86B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a86C$averages alpha-carboxyl and alpha-amino, ignoring the ionizable side chain$a86C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · medium · Skill 4 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans A] (g87)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt87$Lysine has three ionizable groups with \(\text{pK}_a\) values of \(2.2\) (\(\alpha\)-carboxyl), \(9.0\) (\(\alpha\)-amino), and \(10.5\) (side-chain \(\varepsilon\)-amino). What is the approximate isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)) of lysine?$qt87$,
    $op87$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(9.75\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(7.2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(6.4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(5.6\\)"}]$op87$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex87$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The isoelectric point is the pH at which the molecule carries no net charge, and it is computed as the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the neutral (net-zero) species. The answer to this question is A because for a basic amino acid such as lysine the net-zero form is bracketed by the two highest \(\text{pK}_a\) values. Walking up the titration: at very low pH lysine carries \(+2\) (carboxyl protonated, both amino groups protonated). Deprotonating the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\,2.2\)) gives \(+1\); deprotonating the \(\alpha\)-amino (\(\text{pK}_a\,9.0\)) gives the net-zero species; deprotonating the \(\varepsilon\)-amino (\(\text{pK}_a\,10.5\)) gives \(-1\). The neutral species therefore lies between \(\text{pK}_a\,9.0\) and \(\text{pK}_a\,10.5\), so \(\text{pI}=(9.0+10.5)/2=9.75\). (Choice B) \(7.2\) is the average of all three \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.2+9.0+10.5)/3=7.23\); \(\text{pI}\) is never the mean of every ionizable group, only of the two that bracket the neutral form. (Choice C) \(6.4\) averages the lowest and highest \(\text{pK}_a\), \((2.2+10.5)/2=6.35\), pairing the two extreme groups rather than the two adjacent groups that straddle the net-zero species. (Choice D) \(5.6\) averages the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\), \((2.2+9.0)/2=5.6\); that pairing is correct for an acidic or neutral amino acid but uses the wrong pair for a basic side chain, whose extra positive group shifts the neutral window upward. Skill 4$ex87$,
    'medium', '5D', $cc87$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc87$, 'biochemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a87B$averages all three pKa values instead of the two flanking the neutral species$a87B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a87C$averages the two extreme (lowest and highest) pKa rather than the two adjacent ones bracketing net-zero$a87C$ FROM q
