-- Q6 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · medium · Skill 1 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans C] (g88)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt88$Aspartate is titrated from low to high pH and shows three distinct ionizations at \(\text{pK}_a\) values of \(2.0\), \(3.9\), and \(9.9\). Compared with glycine titrated under the same conditions, how many equivalence points does the aspartate curve display, and which structural feature accounts for the difference?$qt88$,
    $op88$[{"label":"A","text":"Two equivalence points, the same as glycine, because only the \\(\\alpha\\)-amino and \\(\\alpha\\)-carboxyl groups titrate while the side chain stays protonated throughout."},{"label":"B","text":"One equivalence point, fewer than glycine, because the two carboxyl groups deprotonate together as a single ionization near \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) \\(3\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"Three equivalence points, one more than glycine, because the ionizable acidic side-chain carboxyl adds a third titratable proton beyond the \\(\\alpha\\)-amino and \\(\\alpha\\)-carboxyl groups."},{"label":"D","text":"Four equivalence points, two more than glycine, because each \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) plus the isoelectric point each register as a separate equivalence point."}]$op88$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex88$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is C because aspartate carries three titratable protons, one on each ionizable group, so its titration curve shows three equivalence points, whereas glycine has only two ionizable groups and therefore two equivalence points. The number of equivalence points equals the number of ionizable protons: aspartate has the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(2.0\)), the side-chain \(\beta\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(3.9\)), and the \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a\) \(9.9\)) for a total of three, while glycine has only an \(\alpha\)-carboxyl and an \(\alpha\)-amino group for a total of two. The extra titratable proton, and thus the third equivalence point, comes from the ionizable acidic side chain that glycine lacks; each equivalence point sits between two adjacent buffering regions where the curve rises steeply after one group has been fully deprotonated. (Choice A) The acidic side-chain carboxyl of aspartate does titrate within the experimental pH range (its \(\text{pK}_a\) is \(3.9\), well below physiological and titration endpoints), so it cannot remain protonated throughout; this is the equivalence-point count of a neutral amino acid like glycine and ignores the third ionizable group. (Choice B) The two carboxyl groups have distinct \(\text{pK}_a\) values (\(2.0\) and \(3.9\)) and deprotonate sequentially in separate ionization steps, each producing its own equivalence point, rather than merging into a single combined ionization. (Choice D) The isoelectric point is not an equivalence point; \(\text{pI}\) is computed as the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values flanking the neutral species and falls partway through a buffering region, so only the three proton-transfer steps register as equivalence points. Skill 1$ex88$,
    'medium', '5D', $cc88$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc88$, 'biochemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a88A$neutral-amino-acid equivalence-point count applied to a triprotic side chain$a88A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a88B$two distinct carboxyl ionizations merged into one equivalence point$a88B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a88D$isoelectric point miscounted as an additional equivalence point$a88D$ FROM q;

-- Q7 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · hard · Skill 4 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans D] (g89)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt89$Free histidine sits in a buffered solution at pH 5.0. Using its group \(\text{pK}_a\) values — \(\alpha\)-carboxyl \(\approx 1.8\), imidazole side chain \(\approx 6.0\), and \(\alpha\)-amino \(\approx 9.2\) — what net charge does the predominant form of the molecule carry at this pH?$qt89$,
    $op89$[{"label":"A","text":"A net charge of \\(-1\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"A net charge of \\(0\\) (a neutral zwitterion)"},{"label":"C","text":"A net charge of \\(+2\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"A net charge of \\(+1\\)"}]$op89$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex89$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is D because at pH 5.0 the carboxyl is deprotonated while both nitrogen-bearing groups remain protonated, summing to a net cationic charge of \(+1\). Assign each group by comparing the solution pH to its \(\text{pK}_a\): a group is mostly protonated when \(\text{pH} < \text{pK}_a\) and mostly deprotonated when \(\text{pH} > \text{pK}_a\). The \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 1.8\)) has \(\text{pH} 5.0 > 1.8\), so it is deprotonated to \(\text{COO}^-\), contributing \(-1\). The imidazole side chain (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 6.0\)) has \(\text{pH} 5.0 < 6.0\), so it is largely protonated; by Henderson–Hasselbalch the protonated-to-deprotonated ratio is \(10^{(6.0-5.0)} = 10{:}1\) (about \(91\%\) protonated), contributing \(+1\). The \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 9.2\)) has \(\text{pH} 5.0 \ll 9.2\), so it is protonated to \(\text{NH}_3^+\), contributing \(+1\). The sum is \((-1)+(+1)+(+1) = +1\). This is consistent with the isoelectric point, which for this basic amino acid is the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values flanking the neutral species, \(\text{pI} = (6.0 + 9.2)/2 = 7.6\); because the solution pH of 5.0 lies below \(\text{pI}\), histidine must be net positive. (Choice A) A net charge of \(-1\) follows from treating the imidazole side chain as deprotonated, but \(\text{pH} 5.0\) is below its \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(6.0\), so the side chain is in fact mostly protonated and carries \(+1\), not \(0\); reversing that group's protonation state inverts the result. (Choice B) A net charge of \(0\) assumes the standard zwitterion picture of an amino acid bearing only \(\text{COO}^-\) and \(\text{NH}_3^+\), but that ignores the ionizable imidazole side chain, which at \(\text{pH} 5.0\) is protonated and adds a third charged group of \(+1\). (Choice C) A net charge of \(+2\) is obtained by counting both nitrogen groups as \(+1\) but failing to deprotonate the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl; since \(\text{pH} 5.0\) is well above the carboxyl \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(1.8\), that group is \(\text{COO}^-\) and supplies \(-1\), lowering the total from \(+2\) to \(+1\). Skill 4$ex89$,
    'hard', '5D', $cc89$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc89$, 'biochemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
