-- Q8 [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · hard · Skill 4 · 5D Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules · biochemistry · ans B] (g90)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point',
    $qt90$Arginine (\(\text{p}K_{a1}\approx 2.2\) for \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\), \(\text{p}K_{a2}\approx 9.0\) for \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\), \(\text{p}K_{aR}\approx 12.5\) for the guanidinium side chain; \(\text{pI}\approx 10.8\)) is dissolved in a buffer held at physiological pH 7.4 and run in an electrophoresis cell. Toward which electrode does the dominant arginine species migrate, and why?$qt90$,
    $op90$[{"label":"A","text":"Toward the anode, because at pH 7.4 the molecule carries a net charge of \\(-1\\) and behaves as an anion."},{"label":"B","text":"Toward the cathode, because pH 7.4 lies below the \\(\\text{pI}\\), leaving the molecule net \\(+1\\) and cationic."},{"label":"C","text":"It does not migrate appreciably, because at pH 7.4 the neutral zwitterion with net charge zero predominates."},{"label":"D","text":"Toward the anode, because all three ionizable groups are deprotonated, giving a fully anionic species."}]$op90$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex90$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is B because the buffer pH (7.4) sits well below arginine's isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\approx 10.8\)), and any amino acid placed in a solution more acidic than its \(\text{pI}\) carries a net positive charge and therefore migrates toward the cathode (the negative electrode). Work the charge group by group at pH 7.4: the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{p}K_{a}\approx 2.2\)) is far above its \(\text{p}K_a\), so it is deprotonated, contributing \(-1\); the \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{p}K_{a}\approx 9.0\)) is below its \(\text{p}K_a\), so it stays protonated, contributing \(+1\); and the guanidinium side chain (\(\text{p}K_{a}\approx 12.5\)) is far below its \(\text{p}K_a\), so it remains protonated, contributing \(+1\). Summing: \(-1 + 1 + 1 = +1\). The net charge is \(+1\), confirming a cationic species that moves toward the cathode. (As a check, the \(\text{pI}\) for a basic amino acid is the average of the two highest \(\text{p}K_a\) values, \((9.0 + 12.5)/2 = 10.75\approx 10.8\), and the molecule is neutral only at that pH.) (Choice A) This inverts the charge: it correctly notes that being on one side of the \(\text{pI}\) sets the sign of migration but assigns net \(-1\), which would require pH above the \(\text{pI}\). At pH 7.4 the two basic groups are still protonated, so the net charge is \(+1\), not \(-1\), and the species is a cation, not an anion. (Choice C) The neutral zwitterion (net charge zero) dominates only at the \(\text{pI}\), here near pH 10.8; at pH 7.4 the solution is far more acidic than the \(\text{pI}\), so the protonated, net \(+1\) form predominates and the molecule does migrate. Assuming the zwitterion always predominates at physiological pH is a common misreading of how charge depends on the gap between pH and \(\text{pI}\). (Choice D) Full deprotonation of all three groups, including the guanidinium side chain, would require pH well above 12.5; at pH 7.4 only the \(\alpha\text{-carboxyl}\) is deprotonated while both nitrogen-bearing groups stay protonated, so the species is net \(+1\), not a fully deprotonated anion. Skill 4$ex90$,
    'hard', '5D', $cc90$Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules$cc90$, 'biochemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a90A$Sign of net charge inverted: treats pH below pI as if it produced an anion migrating to the anode$a90A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a90C$Zwitterion-always-dominates fallacy: assumes net-zero neutral form predominates at physiological pH regardless of the pH-vs-pI gap$a90C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a90D$Over-deprotonation: skips the group-by-group protonation check and assumes the high-pKa guanidinium is deprotonated at pH 7.4$a90D$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · easy · Skill 1 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans D] (g91)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt91$The bicarbonate buffer can stabilize blood pH only if dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) is interconverted with carbonic acid (\(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\)) fast enough to keep pace with metabolic acid production. In red blood cells and at the pulmonary capillaries, which enzyme catalyzes this rapid interconversion of \(\text{CO}_2\) and \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\)?$qt91$,
    $op91$[{"label":"A","text":"No enzyme is involved; the hydration of \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) is already fast enough on its own"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lactate dehydrogenase"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic anhydrase"}]$op91$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex91$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The answer to this question is D because carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide, \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), accelerating a reaction that is otherwise far too slow to buffer the rapid changes in acid load the body experiences; this catalysis lets the bicarbonate system reach equilibrium within seconds, which is essential to its role in blood-pH homeostasis. (Choice A) The uncatalyzed hydration of \(\text{CO}_2\) is far too slow to keep the bicarbonate buffer at equilibrium on a physiological timescale; without carbonic anhydrase the system could not respond quickly enough, which is exactly why the enzyme is required. (Choice B) Hemoglobin transports oxygen, carries some carbon dioxide as carbamino groups, and buffers protons, but it does not catalyze the hydration of \(\text{CO}_2\) to carbonic acid; that specific catalytic role belongs to carbonic anhydrase. (Choice C) Lactate dehydrogenase interconverts pyruvate and lactate during anaerobic metabolism and plays no part in the \(\text{CO}_2\)/\(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) interconversion, so it cannot serve this buffering function. Skill 1$ex91$,
    'easy', '3B', $cc91$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc91$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a91A$assumes uncatalyzed CO2 hydration is fast enough, so no enzyme is needed$a91A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a91B$names a blood protein involved in gas transport but not in catalyzing CO2 hydration$a91B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a91C$names an unrelated metabolic enzyme (anaerobic glycolysis)$a91C$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · easy · Skill 2 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans B] (g92)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt92$During intense sprinting, lactic acid pours free \(\text{H}^+\) into a runner's blood faster than the lungs or kidneys can react. Considering only the bicarbonate equilibrium \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\), how does it respond in the first seconds, before any organ compensation?$qt92$,
    $op92$[{"label":"A","text":"The equilibrium shifts to the right, generating additional \\(\\text{H}^+\\) to oppose the acid that was added."},{"label":"B","text":"The equilibrium shifts to the left, with the added \\(\\text{H}^+\\) combining with \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) to form \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3\\) and then \\(\\text{CO}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\), consuming some of the added \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and blunting the pH drop."},{"label":"C","text":"The equilibrium does not move because the buffer holds blood pH fixed at exactly 7.4 regardless of how much \\(\\text{H}^+\\) is added."},{"label":"D","text":"The equilibrium shifts left and \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) concentration rises as fresh bicarbonate is generated to absorb the added acid."}]$op92$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex92$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The answer to this question is B because Le Chatelier's principle predicts that adding a stress to one side of an equilibrium drives the reaction toward the opposite side; the lactic acid adds the product \(\text{H}^+\) to the right side of \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\), so the system shifts left. As it shifts left, the added \(\text{H}^+\) combines with \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) to make \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which proceeds to \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}\); this removes a portion of the free \(\text{H}^+\) from solution, so the pH falls less than it otherwise would. That immediate chemical buffering happens before the lungs blow off the extra \(\text{CO}_2\) or the kidneys excrete acid and regenerate bicarbonate. (Choice A) A rightward shift would produce more \(\text{H}^+\), which adds to the very stress just imposed rather than relieving it; this reverses the actual direction of the Le Chatelier response. (Choice C) A buffer resists but does not abolish pH change, so adding \(\text{H}^+\) does lower the pH and does move the equilibrium; pH is not pinned at 7.4 by the buffer alone. (Choice D) Although the shift is leftward, \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) is consumed in that step as it mops up the added \(\text{H}^+\), so its concentration falls rather than rises; new bicarbonate generation is a later renal process, not the immediate equilibrium response. Skill 2$ex92$,
    'easy', '3B', $cc92$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc92$, 'biology', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a92A$Shifts equilibrium the wrong way, claiming a rightward shift relieves an added-H+ stress$a92A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a92C$Believes a buffer fixes pH exactly, pinning blood at 7.4 with no equilibrium shift$a92C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a92D$Confuses immediate consumption of bicarbonate with the later renal generation of new bicarbonate$a92D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · medium · Skill 2 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans C] (g93)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt93$A patient in metabolic acidosis (arterial pH 7.28) responds by breathing rapidly and deeply, lowering arterial \(\text{PCO}_2\). For the equilibrium \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\), which effect does this hyperventilation most likely have on the blood?$qt93$,
    $op93$[{"label":"A","text":"Exhaling \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) shifts the equilibrium to the right, generating additional \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and lowering the blood pH further."},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid breathing adds \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) and \\(\\text{O}_2\\) to the blood, raising \\([\\text{H}^+]\\) and dropping the pH."},{"label":"C","text":"Removing \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) shifts the equilibrium to the left, consuming \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and raising the pH toward 7.4."},{"label":"D","text":"Removing \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) shifts the equilibrium to the left, yet the loss of \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3\\) lowers the pH."}]$op93$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex93$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The carbonic acid–bicarbonate buffer couples blood pH to ventilation through \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\). The answer to this question is C because exhaling \(\text{CO}_2\) lowers \(\text{PCO}_2\), and by Le Chatelier's principle the system replaces the lost \(\text{CO}_2\) by shifting toward the left, a direction that consumes \(\text{H}^+\); a falling \([\text{H}^+]\) is by definition a rising pH, moving the acidemic blood back toward 7.4. Tracing the reaction: removing the rightmost-feeding species \(\text{CO}_2\) depletes \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which is regenerated from \(\text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\) recombining; each recombination removes one free \(\text{H}^+\), so \([\text{H}^+]\) drops and pH \(= -\log[\text{H}^+]\) climbs. This is the appropriate respiratory compensation a healthy ventilatory drive mounts against a metabolic (non-respiratory) acid load. (Choice A) This reverses the direction of the shift: lowering a reactant-side species pulls the equilibrium toward that side (left), not toward the products, so \(\text{H}^+\) is consumed rather than generated and the pH rises rather than falls. (Choice B) Hyperventilation removes \(\text{CO}_2\) from the blood rather than adding it; while increased ventilation does raise \(\text{O}_2\) uptake, \(\text{O}_2\) is not part of this buffer equilibrium, and the net effect of blowing off \(\text{CO}_2\) is a fall, not a rise, in \([\text{H}^+]\). (Choice D) This correctly identifies a leftward shift but draws the wrong conclusion about pH: a leftward shift consumes \(\text{H}^+\), and a lower \([\text{H}^+]\) raises pH; the transient decrease in \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) does not acidify the blood because it is accompanied by net removal of free protons. Skill 2$ex93$,
    'medium', '3B', $cc93$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc93$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a93A$exhaling CO2 shifts equilibrium right and lowers pH (direction of shift and pH change both inverted)$a93A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a93B$hyperventilation adds CO2/O2 to blood and raises H+$a93B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a93D$correct leftward shift but wrong pH direction (concludes pH falls)$a93D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · medium · Skill 2 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans D] (g94)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt94$A patient brought in after an opioid overdose is hypoventilating, so the lungs clear far less \(\text{CO}_2\) per minute and \(\text{CO}_2\) accumulates in the arterial blood. Before any renal response has had time to occur, how does this rising arterial \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) most directly affect the bicarbonate equilibrium and blood pH?$qt94$,
    $op94$[{"label":"A","text":"Retained \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) pulls \\(\\text{CO}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+ + \\text{HCO}_3^-\\) to the left, consuming \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and raising blood pH."},{"label":"B","text":"Because \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) is a chemically inert gas rather than an acid, retaining it cannot shift the equilibrium and blood pH stays unchanged."},{"label":"C","text":"The kidneys immediately excrete the extra \\(\\text{H}^+\\) as fast as it forms, holding blood pH at \\(7.4\\) despite the retained \\(\\text{CO}_2\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Retained \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) drives \\(\\text{CO}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+ + \\text{HCO}_3^-\\) to the right, generating more \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and lowering blood pH."}]$op94$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex94$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The answer to this question is D because a primary fall in alveolar ventilation retains \(\text{CO}_2\), and dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) is an acidic gas that feeds the carbonic-acid/bicarbonate equilibrium. Hypoventilation slows the exhalation that normally removes metabolic \(\text{CO}_2\), so arterial \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) rises. By Le Chatelier's principle, adding \(\text{CO}_2\) (a reactant) to \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\) pushes the reaction to the right, increasing both \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) and \(\text{H}^+\). The added \(\text{H}^+\) lowers pH, which is the defining feature of a primary respiratory acidosis. The Henderson-Hasselbalch form \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_a + \log\frac{[\text{HCO}_3^-]}{0.03\,P_{\text{CO}_2}}\) shows the same result: with no time yet for the kidneys to change \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\), raising the denominator term \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) shrinks the ratio and lowers pH. (Choice A) This reverses the direction of the shift. Adding \(\text{CO}_2\) as a reactant drives the equilibrium toward products, not toward reactants, so \(\text{H}^+\) is produced rather than consumed and pH falls rather than rises; consuming \(\text{H}^+\) and raising pH would describe \(\text{CO}_2\) removal (hyperventilation), the opposite disturbance. (Choice B) Dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) is not inert: it reacts with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates to release \(\text{H}^+\), so \(\text{CO}_2\) functions as a volatile acid and retaining it necessarily perturbs the equilibrium and blood pH. (Choice C) Renal \(\text{H}^+\) excretion and \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) reabsorption are slow compensatory processes that take hours to days, so they cannot blunt the immediate equilibrium response; the kidneys do not act instantaneously, and conflating delayed renal compensation with the immediate chemistry leaves the acute drop in pH unaccounted for. Skill 2$ex94$,
    'medium', '3B', $cc94$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc94$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a94A$retained-CO2-shifts-equilibrium-left-and-raises-pH$a94A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a94B$CO2-treated-as-inert-non-acid-gas$a94B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a94C$instant-renal-H+-excretion-conflated-with-immediate-equilibrium$a94C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · medium · Skill 4 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans B] (g95)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt95$A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has held a primary respiratory acidosis for several days, with a persistently elevated arterial \(\text{PCO}_2\) and a blood pH that began below 7.4. Given that the disturbance is now sustained, which renal response over the following hours to days would best return the pH toward 7.4?$qt95$,
    $op95$[{"label":"A","text":"The kidneys excrete more \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) and retain \\(\\text{H}^+\\), lowering plasma \\([\\text{HCO}_3^-]\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"The kidneys reabsorb and generate more \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) while excreting more \\(\\text{H}^+\\), raising plasma \\([\\text{HCO}_3^-]\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"The lungs, not the kidneys, carry out this slow days-long adjustment by further altering ventilation"},{"label":"D","text":"The kidneys reabsorb and generate more \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\), but this raises plasma \\([\\text{HCO}_3^-]\\) within minutes"}]$op95$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex95$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The answer to this question is B because, in a sustained primary respiratory acidosis, the kidneys compensate by reabsorbing filtered \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) and generating new \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) while secreting and excreting more \(\text{H}^+\), which raises plasma \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\) to offset the chronically high \(\text{PCO}_2\) and pull pH back toward 7.4. Reading the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship for the bicarbonate system, \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_a + \log\frac{[\text{HCO}_3^-]}{(0.03)(\text{PCO}_2)}\): the elevated \(\text{PCO}_2\) (from \(\text{CO}_2\) accumulation, where \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\)) enlarges the denominator and depresses pH. To restore the ratio, the numerator \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\) must rise, which is exactly what renal \(\text{H}^+\) excretion and \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) generation accomplish. Critically, this renal mechanism is slow: it takes hours to begin and roughly 3 to 5 days to fully develop, which is why it is the appropriate compensatory pathway for a disturbance that has already persisted for several days.

(Choice A) Excreting \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) and retaining \(\text{H}^+\) would lower plasma \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\) and drive pH down further, deepening the acidosis. This is the renal response appropriate to a metabolic alkalosis (high pH), applied in reverse to an acidosis; the direction of \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) handling is exactly opposite to what compensation requires here.

(Choice C) The slow, days-long buffering adjustment in this scenario is renal, not pulmonary. The lungs provide the fast respiratory response (minutes) by changing ventilation, but here the lungs are the diseased origin of the primary disturbance and cannot blow off the retained \(\text{CO}_2\); attributing the slow compensation to the lungs confuses the rapid respiratory mechanism with the slow renal one.

(Choice D) The kidneys do correctly reabsorb and generate \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) to raise plasma \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\), but this does not occur within minutes. Renal compensation begins over hours and reaches its full effect only over about 3 to 5 days; the minutes timescale belongs to the respiratory (ventilatory) response, so the bicarbonate direction is right while the stated timescale is wrong. Skill 4$ex95$,
    'medium', '3B', $cc95$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc95$, 'biology', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
