UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a95C$Confuses the slow renal compensatory mechanism with the fast respiratory one, assigning the days-long bicarbonate adjustment to the lungs rather than the kidneys$a95C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a95D$Identifies the correct organ and direction of bicarbonate handling but mis-states the timescale as minutes instead of the actual hours-to-days of renal compensation$a95D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · hard · Skill 4 · 3B Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems · biology · ans A] (g96)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology',
    $qt96$A classic buffer resists pH change most effectively when the solution pH lies within roughly one unit of the buffer's \(\text{pK}_a\). The carbonic-acid/bicarbonate pair has a \(\text{pK}_a\) near \(6.1\), yet arterial blood is held at pH \(7.4\) — more than one unit above that \(\text{pK}_a\). Given this offset, why does the \(\text{HCO}_3^-/\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\) pair nonetheless serve as the body's dominant extracellular buffer?$qt96$,
    $op96$[{"label":"A","text":"It is an open system: \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) is continuously exhaled by the lungs and \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) is independently regulated by the kidneys, so the acid side can be driven off by ventilation, giving the pair far greater effective capacity than its \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) alone predicts."},{"label":"B","text":"The physiologically relevant \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) of the bicarbonate system is actually \\(7.4\\), so blood pH sits exactly at the point of maximum buffering capacity."},{"label":"C","text":"Blood carries a high \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) concentration, and in a sealed closed system that large reservoir of conjugate base alone is what makes the pair effective at pH \\(7.4\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Operating more than one unit above its \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) is itself advantageous, because the farther blood pH lies from the \\(\\text{pK}_a\\), the greater a buffer's resistance to pH change becomes."}]$op96$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex96$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. A buffer's intrinsic capacity, judged purely from the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship, is indeed greatest near its \(\text{pK}_a\) and falls off as pH moves away; at pH \(7.4\) the bicarbonate pair sits about \(1.3\) units above its \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(6.1\), where a closed-system buffer would already be well past its most effective range. The answer to this question is A because the bicarbonate system is an open system rather than a sealed one. The acid member of the pair, \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), is in rapid equilibrium with dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\), which the lungs continuously exhale; the conjugate base, \(\text{HCO}_3^-\), is independently retained or excreted by the kidneys. Because the two members are regulated by separate organs, the body can drive off the acid side through ventilation and adjust the base side through renal handling instead of being limited to the fixed ratio a closed buffer must obey. When acid is added, the resulting \(\text{CO}_2\) is simply breathed out, so the system behaves as though it has an essentially limitless reservoir on the acid side. This open-system regulation is what gives the pair its dominant buffering role despite the unfavorable \(\text{pK}_a\) offset; the offset matters far less once the system is no longer constrained to a sealed equilibrium. (Choice B) The \(\text{pK}_a\) of the carbonic-acid/bicarbonate system is approximately \(6.1\), not \(7.4\); blood pH does not coincide with the \(\text{pK}_a\), so the claim that the pair sits at its point of maximum closed-system capacity is factually incorrect. (Choice C) Blood does carry a relatively high \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) concentration, but attributing the effectiveness solely to that concentration in a closed system misses the actual mechanism: a sealed buffer more than a unit from its \(\text{pK}_a\) would still be poorly positioned, and it is the open-system removal of \(\text{CO}_2\) by the lungs, not concentration alone, that supplies the extra capacity. (Choice D) This inverts the buffer-capacity relationship; capacity is maximal at the \(\text{pK}_a\) and decreases as pH moves away from it, so lying more than one unit above the \(\text{pK}_a\) reduces, rather than enhances, the intrinsic resistance to pH change. Skill 4$ex96$,
    'hard', '3B', $cc96$Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems$cc96$, 'biology', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
